  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The ninth inning arrived with nothing to show, and that's when Jonah Heim reminded them games aren't over until the final out. Four runs scored after Heim's single. A sharp correction to what had been a blunt loss up to that point. The Athletics got runners on base all night, converting five of their RISP opportunities into runs, but Jeffrey Springs couldn't hold the line. He exited after 4.2 innings with four earned runs charged to his name, and the bullpen. Juenger giving up three in two-thirds, Morris holding two across 2.2. Left them chasing a game they should have been ahead in. They scraped closer but never caught up. Twelve hits and still down by three when it ended. That's the gap between contact and conversion.",
    "what_this_means": "This team doesn't fold early, but it struggles to finish when it matters. Springs couldn't eat innings, the defense couldn't lock the game down early, and the bats needed eight frames to build any real pressure. By then the margin was already baked in. The ninth-inning rally was real enough: Heim started something, the lineup pushed, they made Minnesota work for it. That's the thread worth holding. They don't go away quietly. But quiet persistence isn't a plan when you're 15 games back and losing to a contender at home. The pattern is consistent: they generate offense, leave enough runners on to build a second game, and time runs out before they can fully cash what they earned. Five runs from RISP chances meant something; six runs allowed meant everything. They had chances all night. None of them landed when they had to.",
    "what_to_watch": "Taj Bradley's season ERA sits at 3.90 when the Athletics go back after Minnesota tonight. That's a test right after a lead they couldn't hold.",
